'''''Hyparrhenia hirta''''' is a species of [[Poaceae|grass]] known by the common names '''common thatching grass''' and '''Coolatai grass'''. It is native to much of Africa and Eurasia, and it is known on other continents as an [[introduced species]]. In eastern [[Australia]] it is a tenacious [[noxious weed]].<ref>[http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Hyparrhenia~hirta New South Wales Flora]</ref><ref>Chejara, V.K., et al. (2008). Factors affecting germination of Coolatai grass (''Hyparrhenia hirta''). ''Weed Science'' 56:4 543–48.</ref> In [[South Africa]], where it is native, it is very common and one of the most widely used [[thatching]] grasses.<ref name=afr>[http://www.plantzafrica.com/planthij/hyparrhirta.htm PlantZAfrica.com]</ref><ref>Bussmann, R. W., et al. (2006). [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1475560/ Plant use of the Maasai of Sekenani Valley, Maasai Mara, Kenya.] ''J Ethnobiol Ethnomed'' 2 22.</ref> It is also used for [[grazing]] livestock and weaving mats and [[basket weaving|baskets]].<ref name=afr/>

This is a perennial grass forming clumps {{Convert|30|cm}} to {{Convert|1|m}} tall with tough, dense bases sprouting from [[rhizome]]s. The [[inflorescence]] atop the wiry stem is a [[panicle]] of hairy spikelets with bent [[Awn (botany)|awns]] up to {{Convert|3.5|cm}} long. The grass can grow in a variety of habitat types, in dry conditions, heavy, rocky, [[erosion|eroded]] soils, and disturbed areas.

